INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Wirecard supervisory implications study", "source_url": "https://safe-frankfurt.de/fileadmin/user_upload/editor_common/Policy_Center/SAFE_Policy_White_Paper_74.pdf", "text": "SRC-002 reports that the European Parliament Economic Governance Support Unit study (November 2020; manuscript completed May 2020) assessed the Wirecard case. Its executive summary says, in the authors' view, all five lines of defence failed in some way: Wirecard's internal control system, supervisory board, external audit, oversight bodies for financial reporting and auditing, and market supervisor BaFin. It says there had been signals suggesting fraudulent behaviour years before revelation. The study's preface states: Wirecard faced accounting-consistency allegations from 2015, a 2016 Zatarra report accused it of money laundering and fraud, BaFin requested a FREP investigation in January 2019 after an FT report, BaFin prohibited increases in net short positions for two months and filed complaints against short sellers and the journalist, and FT continued reporting on allegedly inflated sales and profits. KPMG was hired in October 2019; its report, delayed until April 2020, stated KPMG was unable to verify the existence of €1 billion in foreign bank accounts. BaFin filed a criminal complaint in June 2020 and days later Wirecard filed for bankruptcy, acknowledging €1.9 billion in missing financial assets. SRC-002 also frames the causes as a mix of institutional deficiencies, professional negligence and perpetrator moral failures, while warning against hindsight bias. It identifies weaknesses in information flows, external audits/internal controls/market forces, supervisor investigation and enforcement powers, and fragmented European oversight. The authors' assessment is not necessarily the official position of the European Parliament."}
